   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the penal law, in relation to hate crimes and community centers",
    "basePrintNo": "S5512",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S5512-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 52,
       "fullName": "Fredrick J. Akshar II",
       "imgName": "735_Fredrick_Akshar.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 913,
       "sessionMemberId": 952,
       "sessionYear": 2017,
       "shortName": "AKSHAR"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §§240.00 & 485.05, Pen L",
    "lawSection": "Penal Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5512\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the penal law, in relation to hate\ncrimes and community centers\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nTo include threats to community centers, specifically placing a false\nbomb or hazardous substance in the first degree and falsely reporting\nan incident in the second degree to the list of specified offenses\nunder the hate crime law.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 amends section 240.00 of the penal law to explicitly state\nthat in connection with offenses against the public order, a public\nplace includes community centers.\n\nSection 2 amends subdivision 3 of section 485.05 of the penal law to\nadd the crimes of placing a false bomb or hazardous substance in the\nfirst degree and falsely reporting an incident in the second degree to\nthe list of specified offenses, a required element of a hate crime.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nSince the beginning of 2017, over 100 bomb threats have been called in\nto Jewish community centers across the country. These threats go hand\nin hand with a recent dramatic rise in anti-Semitic crime. These\nheinous acts are placing a strain upon communities and disrupting the\neveryday routine of affected centers, forcing unnecessary evacuations\nof adults, children and infants. More importantly, these threats\ncompromise the safety and well-being of those who work at and go to\nthese community centers.\n\nMaking a bomb threat which targets public places like community\ncenters due to their affiliation with a particular group because of\nrace, religion or religious practice should be included as a specified\noffense under the hate crimes statute, enabling perpetrators to be\nprosecuted for a hate crime.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect on the ninetieth day after it shall have\nbecome a law.\n",
    "multiSponsors": {
